   note:`<h3>Documented record</h3><p>Ashley’s 1823 expedition, the June attack, and Glass’s letter about Gardner’s death are supported by the South Dakota State Historical Society’s account.</p><p>The final sentence is interpretation. It introduces the vocabulary rather than quoting Glass.</p>`},
  {title:'The Attack',skill:'Meaning in context',deck:'Read the situation before the word. Context is your compass.',
   story:`<p>Later in 1823, while hunting or scouting near the Grand River, Glass encountered a grizzly. The animal attacked with a force that later accounts struggled to describe. Members of the party killed the bear, but Glass was left gravely wounded.</p><p>Imagine the silence after the others reach him. No one needs a speech in minute detail to understand that the expedition has changed. A man who could scout ahead now depends on the people around him. Strength, speed, and experience still matter, but new factors have come into play.</p>`,
   note:`<h3>Record and reconstruction</h3><p>The attack near the Grand River and the severity of Glass’s injuries belong to the historical tradition. Exact wounds and some actions vary between retellings.</p><p>The silence and the men’s thoughts in the second paragraph are reconstructed. No diary records that moment.</p>`},
  {title:'Left for Dead',skill:'Controlled retrieval',deck:'The easy certainties are gone. Retrieve the exact phrase that fits each situation.',
   story:`<p>The party carried Glass for a time, but his injuries made travel slow. Two men were assigned to remain with him until he died and then bury him. They eventually left, taking his rifle and other equipment. When Glass regained enough awareness to understand his situation, the expedition was gone.</p><p>Would that it were possible to reduce what followed to courage alone. His knowledge mattered. So did weather, water, food, luck, and the country itself. The familiar structure of the expedition had been stripped away. He was left to fend for himself.</p>`,
   note:`<h3>Abandonment, with uncertainty</h3><p>Accounts identify John Fitzgerald and a man often called Jim Bridger as the two who stayed behind, though the younger man’s identification has been debated.</p><p>Glass’s thoughts are not documented. The scene is narrative reconstruction built around the widely reported abandonment.</p>`},
  {title:'The Wilderness',skill:'Reformulation & precision',deck:'Survival language is exact. Prepositions, pronouns, and fixed forms matter under pressure.',
   story:`<p>The traditional account describes Glass crawling at first, then finding ways to move farther as his body recovered. He traveled toward Fort Kiowa, near present-day Chamberlain, South Dakota. Later versions supply berries, roots, carcasses, rivers, landmarks, and encounters in minute detail, but the certainty of those details varies.</p><p>What holds the story together is not a list of ingenious tricks. It is the transformation of scale. A river bend becomes a day’s ambition. A little food becomes extraordinary. The things a comfortable traveler might luxuriate in have been stripped away, yet the journey continues.</p>`,
   note:`<h3>A legend takes shape</h3><p>Britannica gives a journey of roughly 200 to 300 miles over about two months, while warning that later details became legendary.</p><p>This page avoids treating every colorful episode as settled fact. The second paragraph interprets the story rather than reporting Glass’s words.</p>`},
  {title:'The Long Way Back',skill:'Application in a narrative',deck:'Build the journey in sequence, then tell it in your own words.',
   story:`<p>Glass did eventually reach Fort Kiowa in the autumn of 1823. That plain arrival is easy to miss inside the enormous legend. The journey has become a story about a body that refused to stop, but it is also a story about direction, knowledge, and dependence on a world beyond one person’s will.</p><p>Now the language becomes yours. Reconstruct the broad sequence, then write an account that does more than admire endurance. Use the target expressions to explain what happened and what the story means.</p>`,